"Britannica describes the castle as a medieval stronghold that was generally the home of the king or lord of the land around it. Strongholds with the same purpose were built in many parts of the world, including Japan and India. This lesson looks mostly at castles in western Europe, where they developed rapidly from the 9th century, and then at great stone buildings elsewhere in the same centuries.": "3d294153332c",
"Britannica describes the castle as a medieval stronghold that was generally the home of the king or lord of the land around it. Strongholds with the same purpose were built in many parts of the world, including Japan and India. This lesson looks mostly at castles in western Europe, where they developed rapidly from the 9th century, and then at great stone buildings elsewhere in the same centuries. The first castles were often made of earth and timber. A mound, the motte, was ringed by a ditch and topped by a tower, and a fenced yard, the bailey, lay at its foot. After the Norman Conquest of England in 1066, well over 500 castles were raised in twenty years to control the conquered land. English Heritage notes that local people were forced to help build them. Timber could burn and rot, so lords rebuilt in stone. The keep became the strongest part of the castle, with a well, private rooms, and stores. Wooden fences were replaced by stone curtain walls with towers, and gates were guarded by drawbridges and portcullises. Sieges were common: in 1215 attackers dug under a corner of Rochester's keep, and in 1216 Dover Castle held out for four months. A castle was also a household and a place of work. The National Archives lists constables, masons, blacksmiths, and servants among the people who kept a castle running. Most castles in England were granted by the king to his loyal lords. The lord's living quarters included a great hall, and records of 1396 at Portchester Castle list a new hall, kitchen, chambers, and offices. Most people did not live in castles. Most were farmers in villages, and in much of western Europe many villages were part of a lord's manor, with peasant houses, a church, a mill, and the lord's manor house, surrounded by fields, meadows, and woods. Peasants paid the lord in labor, money, and produce. Serfs were bound to their land and needed the lord's permission to leave, marry, or change jobs; they had little protection in law and passed to a new lord along with the land. From the 14th century serfdom faded in western Europe, but in eastern Europe it lasted into the 1800s. Great stone buildings rose in other lands too. In Syria, the Hospitaller knights, a European crusading order, built the Crac des Chevaliers from 1142 to 1271, and the Mamluks, Muslim sultans of Egypt and Syria, added to it later. In southern Africa, ancestors of the Shona built the stone walls of Great Zimbabwe, the center of a large state, between about 1100 and 1450, without mortar; scholars think those walls showed the rulers' authority rather than being built for war. In Europe, the age of the medieval castle ended when guns could knock down walls quickly, as French armies showed in Italy in 1494.": "fd90f3b8450e",
