"Great stone buildings rose in other lands too. In Syria, the Hospitaller knights, a European crusading order, built the Crac des Chevaliers from 1142 to 1271, and the Mamluks, Muslim sultans of Egypt and Syria, added to it later. In southern Africa, ancestors of the Shona built the stone walls of Great Zimbabwe, the center of a large state, between about 1100 and 1450, without mortar; scholars think those walls showed the rulers' authority rather than being built for war. In Europe, the age of the medieval castle ended when guns could knock down walls quickly, as French armies showed in Italy in 1494.": "6ad02598a004",
